WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Human night vision provides less color, detail, depth perception,

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.495
and contrast than daylight vision.

00:00:05.495 --> 00:00:09.280
Small lights may be visible while the aircraft body, surrounding obstacles,

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.500
and relative distance remain difficult to judge.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.430
Night adaptation develops gradually and can be disrupted by bright phones,

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:17.500
controller screens, headlights, or spotlights.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.060
Use approved display settings and position equipment to reduce glare without making

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.500
essential information unreadable.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.343
Visual illusions can affect orientation.

00:00:24.343 --> 00:00:28.300
A stationary light may appear to move, distant lights can merge with aircraft lights,

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.500
and a featureless background can remove scale.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:35.000
Do not rely on one light or one visual cue to determine attitude and distance.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.195
Fatigue reduces attention, communication, and reaction time.

00:00:38.195 --> 00:00:40.290
Consider time of day, work duration, cold, travel,

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.464
and repeated missions during the crew briefing.

00:00:42.464 --> 00:00:46.500
A technically qualified pilot may still be unfit for a demanding night mission.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.000
Maintain visual line of sight as required and operationally necessary.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.500
A video feed narrows attention and cannot show every aircraft, obstacle, or person.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.580
A visual observer can help, but roles, calls, viewing position,

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.500
and handoff procedures must be clear.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:03.730
Use brief communications for aircraft location, orientation, hazards, battery, signal,

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:04.500
and landing.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.000
If the pilot loses orientation, stop mission tasks, stabilize, use verified cues,

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.960
and recover or land rather than continuing while uncertain.
